# Modern WordPress Development - Best Practices & Patterns

**Status:** ✅ Core Feature - Modern WordPress (Gutenberg, FSE, Block Patterns)

This document defines Spark's approach to modern WordPress development, including Full Site Editing (FSE), Gutenberg blocks, block patterns, and 10up scaffold alignment.

## Philosophy: Smart Detection + Flexibility + Lean on Core

**Core Principles:**
1. **Smart Detection** - Analyze existing project structure to determine best approach
2. **User Flexibility** - Always allow users to override detected patterns
3. **Lean on Core** - Prioritize WordPress core blocks and patterns over custom solutions
4. **Modern First** - Default to blocks/FSE, support legacy when needed
5. **10up Alignment** - Follow 10up scaffold conventions when detected

## WordPress Project Detection

### Detection Priority & Patterns

When generating WordPress components, Spark follows this detection hierarchy:

```javascript
const detectWordPressProject = () => {
  const patterns = {
    // 10up Scaffold Detection
    tenup: {
      indicators: [
        'mu-plugins/10up-plugin/',
        'themes/10up-theme/',
        'package.json with @10up/toolkit',
        'composer.json with 10up dependencies'
      ],
      structure: {
        functionality: 'mu-plugins/[plugin-name]/',
        presentation: 'themes/[theme-name]/',
        blocks: 'themes/[theme-name]/blocks/',
        components: 'themes/[theme-name]/components/',
        patterns: 'themes/[theme-name]/patterns/'
      }
    },
    
    // Block Theme Detection
    blockTheme: {
      indicators: [
        'themes/[theme]/theme.json',
        'themes/[theme]/templates/',
        'themes/[theme]/parts/',
        'themes/[theme]/patterns/'
      ],
      fseEnabled: true
    },
    
    // Classic Theme Detection
    classicTheme: {
      indicators: [
        'themes/[theme]/functions.php',
        'themes/[theme]/style.css',
        'No theme.json'
      ],
      fseEnabled: false
    },
    
    // Plugin vs Theme Placement
    placement: {
      muPlugins: 'mu-plugins/',
      plugins: 'wp-content/plugins/',
      themes: 'wp-content/themes/'
    }
  };
  
  return analyzeAndRecommend(patterns);
};
```

### Smart Placement Logic

**Decision Tree for Component Placement:**

```
1. Analyze project structure
   ├─ 10up scaffold detected?
   │  ├─ Yes → Use 10up conventions
   │  │  ├─ Functionality → mu-plugins/10up-plugin/
   │  │  ├─ Blocks → themes/10up-theme/blocks/
   │  │  └─ Components → themes/10up-theme/components/
   │  └─ No → Continue analysis
   │
   ├─ Block theme detected?
   │  ├─ Yes → Use FSE structure
   │  │  ├─ Blocks → themes/[theme]/blocks/
   │  │  ├─ Patterns → themes/[theme]/patterns/
   │  │  └─ Parts → themes/[theme]/parts/
   │  └─ No → Classic theme structure
   │
   ├─ Existing components found?
   │  ├─ Yes → Follow existing pattern
   │  └─ No → Ask user for preference
   │
   └─ No clear pattern → Present options to user
```

## Modern WordPress Component Types

### 1. Core Block Patterns (Preferred - Lean on Core!)

**When to use:** Component can be built with core blocks

**Example:** Accordion using core Details/Summary blocks or Group blocks

```bash
# User command
@spark accordion wordpress

# Spark Analysis
🔍 Analyzing component requirements...

Good news! This accordion can be built using WordPress core blocks:
✓ No custom code needed
✓ Works out of the box with FSE
✓ Easier to maintain

**Recommended Approach: Block Pattern**

I'll create a block pattern using core Group and Paragraph blocks
with custom styling for your accordion functionality.

Want to proceed with this approach? Or would you prefer a custom block?

Options:
1. ✅ Block Pattern (uses core blocks) - Recommended
2. Custom Block (full control, more maintenance)
3. Both (pattern for simple, block for advanced)
```

**Generated Files for Block Pattern:**
```
themes/10up-theme/patterns/
└── accordion-faq.php
    ├── Pattern registration
    ├── Core blocks configuration
    └── Custom CSS for styling

themes/10up-theme/assets/css/
└── patterns/
    └── accordion-faq.css
```

### 2. Custom Gutenberg Blocks (When Core Isn't Enough)

**When to use:** Complex functionality, custom data structures, unique interactions

```bash
# User explicitly requests custom block
@spark accordion wordpress --as=block
```

**Generated Files for Custom Block:**
```
themes/10up-theme/blocks/accordion/
├── block.json                 # Block registration (block.json v3)
├── index.js                   # Block registration (JS)
├── edit.js                    # Editor component (React)
├── save.js                    # Frontend save (optional - use render.php)
├── render.php                 # PHP render callback (recommended)
├── view.js                    # Frontend interactions
├── style.scss                 # Frontend + Editor styles
├── editor.scss                # Editor-only styles
├── variations.js              # Block variations
└── README.md                  # Usage documentation
```

### 3. Dynamic Blocks (PHP-Rendered)

**When to use:** Server-side data, PHP logic, ACF integration

```bash
@spark accordion wordpress --dynamic=true --acf=true
```

**Generated Files:**
```
mu-plugins/10up-plugin/blocks/accordion/
├── block.json
├── index.php                  # Block registration
├── render.php                 # PHP render callback
├── acf-fields.json            # ACF field definitions
├── view.js                    # Frontend JavaScript
└── style.scss
```

### 4. Block Variations

**When to use:** Multiple pre-configured versions of same block

```bash
@spark accordion wordpress --variations=faq,product,steps
```

**Generated Files:**
```
themes/10up-theme/blocks/accordion/
├── variations.js
│   ├── FAQ variation
│   ├── Product details variation
│   └── Step-by-step variation
└── block.json (with variations config)
```

## Full Site Editing (FSE) Integration

### Detection & Integration

**FSE Detection Logic:**
```javascript
const detectFSE = () => {
  const indicators = {
    themeJson: 'themes/[theme]/theme.json exists',
    templates: 'themes/[theme]/templates/ exists',
    parts: 'themes/[theme]/parts/ exists',
    patterns: 'themes/[theme]/patterns/ exists'
  };
  
  return indicators.themeJson && indicators.templates;
};
```

### FSE Component Generation

When FSE is detected, generate additional FSE-specific files:

```
themes/10up-theme/
├── theme.json (update with component styles)
